var e=`# CHAPTER 4 — Word and Action

The mohawk put his hand on his friend's cap, spun it around in front of his face, and looked at me with that wide smile, white teeth.

— Hey, man. No offense. This idiot here talks without thinking. But honestly: if being inferior to you means ending up the way we are, and being superior means ending up the way you are... What do you think, bro?

The blue cap answered without even looking at me.

— Inferior!

Green mohawk laughed and let out:

— Inferior too. We already made our choice. Thanks!

The mohawk shook the blue cap's cap again.

— Stop touching my cap, damn it. — the blue cap. — You know I don't like it.

They started changing the subject right there. The blue cap started to get up.

Arnold's group laughed at our conversation, mocked it, messed with me like always. And I was tired. Very tired.

— It's so easy. — I shouted. — It's so easy for a couple of brutes like you, a couple of neanderthals, ignorant, who only know how to fight like savages, to want to humiliate other people. And for what reason — I have more money, I'll have more success, I'm smarter. Just because you have power today, you think you can say whatever you want, do whatever you want. But it won't always be like this. It's not always going to be school. You're not always going to have the power.

That wasn't just for the mohawk. It was for everyone around. For everything that was happening. For that life. — It was one of the things they were going to hear on my video, but I couldn't take it anymore and I said it right there.

The blue cap was already on his feet, adjusting his cap. He spoke without looking at me.

— The loser's comfort: waiting for a better future to fall from the sky.

He turned his head slowly.

— Talking is nice, man. But it's action that makes the difference.

This time the fear didn't come. Just anger. Anger at an inferior like that lecturing me.

And that's when I understood why everyone hated the two of them.

---

## The Elbow

I got out from under Arnold's foot. He lost his balance, started shouting and cursing.

I got up and went toward the two of them. Arnold's group let me — obviously figuring I was about to get beaten.

I grabbed the blue cap by the shoulder. He just looked at my hand. And my hand froze. Then I realized what I was doing. I lowered my arm slowly. I didn't say anything. Dominated by fear, terrified — by the look (how did he do that), by the stories they told about the two of them, by everything I knew they could do to me.

That's when Arnold's group arrived.

Arnold wrapped an arm around my neck, squeezed, and buried a fist in my stomach so I wouldn't forget my place.

— I didn't tell you to get up. Why did you get up, PET? — Then, changing his voice for the two: — Sorry, guys. We're still training our PET. But by the end of the year he'll be properly domesticated. Back to your place, Ryan. Kneel and wag your tail.

For a moment, time stopped.

I heard Arnold from far away. The group no longer existed, the school no longer existed. Only me and the blue cap, our eyes locked. Me doubled over with stomach pain, never taking my eyes off him. Arnold pulled my hair, tried to throw me down, and I didn't look away.

And it was as if his eyes were saying: *ACTION*

Action, as if I could take any action. Everyone decided everything for me. There isn't a single memory, not one act in my entire life, that was my choice, that was an action of my own.

I don't know what came over me. My elbow moved on its own and struck Arnold's chin. He flew backward.

It was the first action I ever took in my life.

Thinking about it now, calmly: the second the elbow landed, the blue cap's lips curved slightly. Almost a smile.

Time started moving again.

— Son of a bitch! — Arnold, mouth bleeding, voice torn. — You hit my mouth. I'm bleeding, you piece of shit. — To the group: — End him.
`;export{e as default};
